{:edb/db-before-t 177 :edb/db-after-t 178 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533490 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:47:52.795Z" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402908 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402915 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402990 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402970 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402977 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402956 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067180 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186069340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402756 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1071 "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186069340 1071 "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186069340 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402963 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186087896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402901 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090604 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186092739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402984 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1071 "If $f(x) = 3^{2x^{2}}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186092739 1071 "If $f(x)={3}^{2{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186092739 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={3}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\cdot (4x) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099621 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186101415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402816 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statement are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nI. The product $AB$ is well defined\nII. The product $BA$ is well defined\nIII. The product $AC$ is well defined" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186101415 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nWhich of the following statement are true regarding the matrices $A, B$, and $C$?\n\n1. The product $AB$ is well defined\n2. The product $BA$ is well defined\n3. The product $AC$ is well defined" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186101415 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that all three statements are true." 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403010 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402997 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402788 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186111177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186108757 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186111177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402822 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186111177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186111177 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{3}$\n2. $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n3. $99999$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186111177 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $\\sqrt{3}$\nII. $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\nIII. $99\\,999$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186111177 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402762 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402769 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114109 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186115867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402881 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115867 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186115867 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, S = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186115867 1074 "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Q$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=7{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are elementary matrices." 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186119850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402860 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186120427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118646 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186120427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402782 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186120427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186120427 1071 "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 11\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186120427 1071 "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 11\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186120427 1074 "The matrix $A$ is an upper triangular matrix since all the entries below the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nThe matrices $B$ and $C$ are *not* upper triangular matrices since they each have a non-zero element below the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{31} & =5\\ne 0 \\\\ {c}_{32} & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402887 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402894 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402928 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402942 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402949 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402935 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402802 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129121 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402809 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128137 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186129781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402776 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1071 "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan θ$?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186129781 1071 "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129781 1074 "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128281 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186129805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402922 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1071 "![](images/f1/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186129805 1071 "![](images/f1/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186129805 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186133811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402749 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402795 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186133918 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402867 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186133918 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402874 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402847 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402834 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160616 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402828 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162559 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i} + kR_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary row operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162559 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{32}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}.$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160628 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402841 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3}$\nII. ${C}_{2}:=…$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162561 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations\n\n1. $C_{1}:=C_{1} + C_{3}$\n2. $C_{2}:=…$\n3. $C_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form $C_{i}:=C_{i} + kC_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the missing elementary column operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162561 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -7 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{3}.$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160634 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402854 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186162562 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_{i}:=C_{i} + kC_{3}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the elementary column operations?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186162562 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}.$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175138 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186176505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403004 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1071 "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = (3t - 5)^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$." 13194139533490 17592186045585 false] [17592186176505 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186176505 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402750 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1069 "q-41751" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1071 "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the year's first five months. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $16$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}78 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1072 #uuid "e98728e8-7708-5748-ae2b-39ed7242bc51" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402749 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}78 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402751 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402752 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402753 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402754 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402755 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1077 "selection" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1079 #edb/ref 17592186402753 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402750 1080 #uuid "dd4ac2e5-74a0-528f-8078-d6aa415b8f9a" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402751 1081 "$$24\\,600$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402751 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402751 1084 #uuid "3ba73043-ba7f-5144-85cc-553d87d248c5" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402752 1081 "$$37\\,300$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402752 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402752 1084 #uuid "1584ef53-01f2-5061-8e58-39b6ee6c83df" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402753 1081 "$$20\\,400$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402753 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402753 1084 #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402754 1081 "$$34\\,000$" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402754 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186402754 1084 #uuid "01465c5f-aea0-556f-b1a7-2a55b583c3af" 13194139533490 