true] [17592186403010 1069 "q-49032" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403010 1071 "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2x+4}$ defined for $x\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403010 1072 #uuid "66a1c6ee-aed3-5592-86e6-e5621e001888" 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403010 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x+4)-2({x}^{3})}{(2x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(-2,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(x+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(-2,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{4(-1)^{3}+12(-1)^{2}}{(2(-1)+4)^{2}}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{9}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403012 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403013 13194139533490 17592186045585 true] [17592186403011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403014 13194139533490 1759218604558